<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Auto Scaling and Types</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f4f8fb;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        h1 {
            text-align: center;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }
        h2 {
            color: #34495e;
            margin-top: 25px;
        }
        .section {
            background: #ffffff;
            padding: 15px;
            margin: 15px 0;
            border-left: 6px solid #3498db;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        ul {
            margin-top: 10px;
        }
        table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin-top: 20px;
            background: #ffffff;
        }
        th, td {
            border:  1px solid #ccc;
            padding: 10px;
            text-align: left;
        }
        th {
            background-color: #3498db;
            color: white;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Auto Scaling and Types of Auto Scaling</h1>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>What is Auto Scaling?</h2>
        <p>Auto Scaling automatically increases or decreases server resources based on traffic demand.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Improves performance</li>
            <li>Reduces downtime</li>
            <li>Saves cost</li>
            <li>Handles traffic spikes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>1️⃣ Based on Scaling Direction</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Vertical Scaling (Scale Up/Down)</b> – Increase CPU or RAM of a server.</li>
            <li><b>Horizontal Scaling (Scale Out/In)</b> – Add or remove servers.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>2️⃣ Based on Trigger Type</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Reactive Scaling</b> – Scales based on live metrics like CPU usage.</li>
            <li><b>Predictive Scaling</b> – Uses historical data to predict traffic.</li>
            <li><b>Scheduled Scaling</b> – Scales at specific times.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>3️⃣ Based on Architecture</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Instance Auto Scaling</b> – Adds/removes virtual machines.</li>
            <li><b>Container Auto Scaling</b> – Scales containers (e.g., Kubernetes pods).</li>
            <li><b>Serverless Auto Scaling</b> – Automatically scales functions.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Description</th>
            <th>Example</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Vertical</td>
            <td>Increase server power</td>
            <td>Upgrade RAM</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Horizontal</td>
            <td>Add more servers</td>
            <td>1 → 5 Servers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Reactive</td>
            <td>CPU-based scaling</td>
            <td>Scale when CPU > 70%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Predictive</td>
            <td>Forecast-based scaling</td>
            <td>Evening traffic spike</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Scheduled</td>
            <td>Time-based scaling</td>
            <td>Scale at 9 AM</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

</body>
</html>
